 this crisis really raises the  question of whether we can do that  differently  and there is potential this i was just  having  a chat conversation here with david  and the idea of building  agri processing industry rural  enterprises  etc as new opportunities  for employing rural populations and  reducing the migrant flow is a way to  think about it and i think it can be  done  um in a more novel way with  smartphone technologies with icts  and social networks and  and other systems coming in now that  you can create much more exciting  employment opportunities  for rural youth today than you could in  the past  so i definitely think policy community  should be thinking about this more  seriously  can i head to that point there  i think if you're planning this world  indeed you'll have a reshift to focus in  rural areas and  rural production more young people going  out there but it's much more to it that  is  more maybe cultural and psychological  because if you look at you know in  africa how many of those people in urban  areas had jobs even before covered right  the easiest of course we've known that's  the law of  and happened they have gone to zimbabwe  last month and a lot of my cousins have  moved from  rural urban areas they've gone back to  rural areas now but  they're still saying is the moment this  is over we're all gonna kind of go back  and we all have this you know even here  in this part of the world that  as soon as this is over we're gonna go  back to the normal  so there is a little bit of a stickiness  to that  lifestyle that would require some  significant  re-education and change for people to  realign and  realize that the new normal is not going  back to  where you were before but we really have  this  mindset that you know anchors us to  opportunities whether they exist or not  in urban areas there's a poor then a law  that's gonna  require quite some education of  especially young people to realize  opportunities could be located somewhere  else but if you could fix it that way i  think this will be a long for long-term  benefit of  both develop food security and  development overall that  we can create opportunities in rural  areas as well  thank you ed rachel do you want to add  to that  i think i i agree that i think  part of the solution is trying to trying  to support  rural areas um there's been a real issue  here  in new york state with slaughterhouses  for example for local livestock  producers having access to adequate  uh places even though there's a rise in  demand they  there hasn't been adequate  slaughterhouse facilities for  local producers to to meet that demand  and that's part of a bigger picture  which is really policies and programs  which have really supported  large-scale producers and large  companies  at the expense of small-scale and  mid-sized producers  and you've really seen that in the  united states and that has hollowed out  uh rural economies um because fewer  people are living in rural places  there's fewer  businesses to support fewer producers  and and it's been devastating  in general for rural america and  and this uh pandemic has highlighted  that  more so i i would agree that supporting  um local and regional food producers and  distributors and  food-related businesses and making these  connections  between local consumers getting access  to healthy  diverse food sources um and and  supporting a regional economy is is a  very important  um learning that we should push for  um but it will require a shift in the  direction of  federal and state policy in general  which has gone in the opposite direction  thank you thank you rachel so we're 15  minutes over now  uh we can probably entertain one more  burning question if there is one from  the floor  anyone like to ask a final closing  question  all right uh with with that uh are there  any parting comments from the panel we  deeply appreciate you coming today it's  really been a treat to hear your  perspectives on this deeply appreciated  on behalf of  uh solo and crop sciences in the sips  community but any  parting comments um this will just  remind everyone this will be recorded so  feel free to  point other people to this that couldn't  make it today  i want to say that to me um this is a  this culvert pandemic is a new  class of uh natural disasters that you  know  best similarities with things that we've  had in the past but it's got some unique  aspects to it  the jury is still far from out so the  stories that we're writing today will  have to be rewritten  three or four times before we can  actually fully do a postmortem on this  so  i'm just aging that these are emerging  lessons and they're far from long-term  lessons  even though we're very quick to want to  make key conclusions out of this  i think we more of the effects will be  written  five years from now more volumes will be  written about this and what he did  to shape the nature of food security  around the world  i think what strikes me is some of the  food security and vulnerability  challenges  that we see in the developing world we  also see right here in new york state  and  and you know our communities are so  disattached that we work on  india or africa or the us but  cross learning is so limited between  what's happening in the u.s and what's  happening in the developing world then  it will be really interesting to build  those bridges  yes i i would agree probably i think  that we could learn a lot from  listening to each other's experiences um  but uh  i just want to thank thank you for  inviting us to this panel  andy and uh it's been a pleasure to talk  and to listen to each other so thank you  thanks again really great presentation  thank you everyone for attending have a  good day all right  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


